# Every conformance rule, against the documents loaded. One row per rule on checks/roster.sqlc:
# whether any row it examined violates it.
#
# The corpus is held to its own rules
#   The rules report; nothing required them to be quiet. `rules.sql` prints violations and fails
#   on none, so a corpus that contradicted the model would still build, and every sentence in this
#   repository that cites a filing as correct would rest on nobody having looked. This law turns
#   the empty violations table into an assertion: the soundness run fails the moment a loaded
#   document, corpus or fixture, is found in breach of a rule.
#
# Not a defect in the machinery, and why it is a law anyway
#   Every other law here says a query computes what it claims to. This one says the documents obey
#   what the queries check, which is a claim about the evidence. It sits on the same roster because
#   it has the same failure mode: a green run that meant nothing, here because a violating document
#   was printed and not read.
#
# Examined is carried beside the verdict
#   A rule that examined nothing passes this law trivially, and reports/coverage.sqlc is where that
#   is judged. The detail carries the count so the two readings sit side by side.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `conforms`.
#   Inside a transaction that rolls back, move `enterprise-contract`'s `support-cover` nameplate
#   from 168 to 100. `nameplate_not_a_multiple` must fail here, with every rule the edit reaches.
-- folds/rule_subjects.sqlc for every rule checks/roster.sqlc declares, one verdict per rule.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT r.slug AS subject,
           f.violated = 0 AS holds,
           format('%s examined, %s violating', f.answered, f.violated) AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
    ) r
    JOIN (
        :compose(folds/rule_subjects.sqlc)
    ) f ON f.subject = r.rule
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'conforms'
